[Music]  my progress has been accelerating in the  past few years as you have heard from  your short stock earlier and there are  many exciting and difficult challenges  that arise with increasing air  capabilities from figuring out how to  specify the complexity of human values  to machines to questions of policy and  governance and how to help our  policymakers manage the societal impacts  of a yet and the good news is that it's  not too early to work on these problems  now and in fact we have many awesome  people who are working in these problems  who are actually right here in this  audience  foi has funded 37 projects in ASAT and  many of those researchers have presented  their work at the workshop that preceded  this conference and over the next 20  minutes I'll talk about some overall  themes in this work and all the really  awesome progress that our researchers  have made we'll start with the question  of value learning how do we specify  complicated and sometimes contradictory  human values to machines the question of  what happens if you don't do a good job  of specifying what you want has been  extensively explored in fiction from  ancient fables like King Midas to  Sorcerer's Apprentice and we all know  what happened when King Midas asked  for everything he touched turned to gold  he got exactly what he asked for but not  what he wanted and I researchers have  some answers to how to provide this kind  of thing  with our AI systems some of our  researchers approach the question of  value learning through human feedback as  he earlier heard from Stuart Russell one  approach is to teach the agent by the  most trading human actions what a human  would do in a given situation and the  co-operative part of the inverse  reinforcement learning method for this  is that we don't just show the agent how  a human would make coffee perfectly but  we actually teach the agent how to make  coffee  Hawaiian Evans would point out that  human actions are often kind of  inconsistent and suboptimal and that  makes them more difficult to learn from  so he works on modifying inverse  reinforcement learning to account for  these human inconsistencies and biases  well cushy on Oh on the other hand would  say that human feedback is great but we  don't want to be giving our artificial  agents feedback every five seconds and  having them ask well is it okay to do  this it's okay to do that every five  seconds so we want we want them to make  use of human feedback much more  efficiently and that goes into the  question of how to make AI control  scaleable and this approach to this is  semi-supervised learning  another cluster of researchers addressed  the question of value learning through  building in moral concepts directly  francesca rossi works on specifying  ethical laws through various sorts of  constraints that depend on the context  that the agent is in on the other hand  medicine connoisseur looks for patterns  in human decision-making for example we  can give humans various sorts of  scenarios and ask them to rate like how  moral the action question was like  suppose a woman threw a stapler at  someone in the audience when they were  snoring while she was getting a talk is  that like our case that's how moral is  this and we could have a whole bunch of  scenarios like this and have sort of  human ratings for for these actions and  from this we can figure out some  relevant features that we can build into  AI systems and adrien Willer asks to  what extent we can make these moral  concepts that humans have more precise  and consistent even before we build them  into AI systems because there's a lot of  these inconsistencies that come up when  we start trying to specify things like  morality precisely for example what the  concept of fairness means to me might  mean something very different to every  single one of you and we need to make  that more precise another big challenge  in AI safety is robust self modification  for AI systems  how can our AI systems improved  themselves modified themselves while  retaining various safety properties that  we care about in other words how can we  prevent dr. Jekyll from turning into mr.  Hyde Romana Kumar approaches this  question using formal verification he  looks at the challenges of self  referential reasoning when agents try to  prove that they or as a future successor  agents will do the right thing and you  run into all sorts of logical paradoxes  like Galeras paradox like the sentence  is false when you want try to do this  kind of proof at the other hand past  owner brink takes a more empirical  approach if he thinks about how we can  get our AI systems to modify themselves  very gradually and test those  modifications excessively so we really  don't want to let dr. Jekyll  take a large swig of potion and turn  into mr. Hyde because it would be a  large modification not only make small  modifications to make the sub  modification of the agent more bounded  anomaly detection is another big  challenge in air safety how can we have  our AI systems recognized when they are  outside of the regime in which they were  trained and generalized appropriately  and conservatively from their past  experience in other words how can  artificial agents air systems act  reasonably in the presence of unknown  unknowns then borrowing an example here  from person yang where your training  data is something like a house but your  test data could be something like a  skyscraper the person's work helps the  air systems make good predictions and  without making any strong assumptions  about where the test data comes from Tom  Dietrich thinks about how to get a part  of the system monitor the rest of the  system and notice when when the system  is extrapolating and what it is outside  its training theater the other hand  machine League cautions us that we  should never do extrapolation because  extrapolation is fairly risky and we  should in fact look at our data and be  able to test whether it's just a normal  data point on adversarial data point  Brian Ziebart agrees with Machine about  the virtues of being pessimistic and  thinking about adversarial scenarios and  he asks what is the worst case that for  what the test data could be like that's  still like reasonably consistent with  the training data  last but certainly not least there are  the questions of how we can help our  policymakers manage the increasingly  tricky societal impacts of AI Heather  rough emphasizes the importance of  meaningful human control of autonomous  weapons and she thinks about how to  define this concept the tactical  operational and a strategic level Peter  sro thinks about autonomous weapons from  the perspective of liability and he asks  who is responsible for their actions and  what do we even mean by things like  autonomy and agency these are all  concepts that can be fairly difficult to  pin down  Marshall party focuses on the issue of  job automation that you heard about from  Eric's talk earlier and he is organizing  an event in Washington DC that will  bring together various key stakeholders  to help the government respond to this  challenge nick bostrom takes a more  general view of policy and he thinks  about what are the relevant desiderata  as we transition into an era of machine  intelligence such as how do we enable  global coordination and how do we make  sure that a I really benefits everyone  these research themes that I just talked  about are only part of the work of our  grantees unfortunately I didn't have  time to talk about the amazing work of  the rest the rest of our researchers and  this is all part of this vast landscape  of ASAT research so actually Richard  from FLI has put together this really  cool map  ASAT topics and how they relate to each  other which you can I encourage you to  play around with that the future of  weather org slash landscape and I think  this really shows how much more there  still remains to be done even given all  the progress we've made so far there is  a growing community of researchers who  are working on all these problems and  this community spans both academia and  Industry and independent researchers and  many of those teams that are here on the  slide didn't truly exist two years ago  so this community has really been  expanding dramatically since Verto Rico  and that has been really inspiring I  would like all the FOI researchers who  are here in the audience to please stand  up and wait but make yourself known I'd  like to thank all of you for the really  amazing work that you've been doing the  past couple of years  you  [Music]

